//! crypto/ed25519_to_x25519.rye — Season G (Cryptography): the birational Edwards↔Montgomery key conversion authored in pure Rye. One
//! keeper's identity is a single Ed25519 key; signing wants that key on the twisted-Edwards curve, and agreeing a shared secret wants it as
//! an X25519 (Montgomery) key. Curve25519 and edwards25519 are the SAME curve in two coordinate systems, birationally equivalent, so one
//! identity key can do both — this rung is the bridge. eddsa_to_x25519 turns an Ed25519 public key into its X25519 public key; x25519_to_eddsa
//! turns a Montgomery u-coordinate back into an Edwards y-encoding; and eddsa_secret_to_x25519 completes the story by deriving the X25519 SECRET
//! from an Ed25519 seed — the clamped SHA-512 scalar the same key already signs with — so one identity holds ONE secret and both signs and agrees.
//! The two field maps add NO new field mathematics: both are one add, one subtract, and one inverse-multiply over the GREEN base field
//! (crypto/fe25519.rye); the secret half names crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's own secret scalar rather than re-deriving it.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G · the crypto parity wave). The map
//! is the identity-unification crux: Kumara's one key both signs (Ed25519, crypto/ed25519_sign.rye) and agrees (X25519, crypto/x25519.rye),
//! so the sealed session need not carry a second, separate key.
//! Kin: crypto/fe25519.rye (the base field beneath both maps, GREEN) · crypto/x25519.rye (the key agreement the forward map feeds) ·
//! crypto/ed25519_sign.rye (the signing the same key serves). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET — its
//! crypto_eddsa_to_x25519 / crypto_x25519_to_eddsa compute the same maps — never a copied line, studied clean-room from RFC 7748 §4.1 and
//! Monocypher's public API (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX — RFC 7748 §4.1 fixes the birational map between the Montgomery u-coordinate and the Edwards y-coordinate: u = (1 + y)/(1 − y) and
//! its inverse y = (u − 1)/(u + 1). An Ed25519 public key encodes y in its low 255 bits with the sign of x in bit 255; the forward map drops
//! that sign bit (from_bytes already masks bit 255), lifts y to Montgomery u, and packs the 32-byte X25519 public key. The reverse map lifts a
//! Montgomery u back to y and packs it with sign bit 0, the canonical form Monocypher returns. The classic checkpoint: the Edwards base point
//! has y = 4/5, and (1 + 4/5)/(1 − 4/5) = 9 — exactly the X25519 base u-coordinate, so eddsa_to_x25519(base) is 9 on the nose.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. The maps run over TEST keys only and call no
//! std.crypto in the primitive itself. Parity is proven at selftest against references runnable now: the published RFC 7748 §4.1 base-point
//! relationship (Edwards base ↔ u = 9), and Zig's own std.crypto — an Edwards public key from std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.basePoint.clampedMul
//! and the X25519 public key from std.crypto.dh.X25519.recoverPublicKey, both over the identical clamped scalar, must satisfy
//! eddsa_to_x25519(ed_pub) = x25519_pub byte-for-byte across a deterministic spread of scalars — two independent implementations pinning the
//! map. A round-trip proves the reverse map inverts the forward one. Monocypher-source parity stays the same held-fetch horizon the other
//! primitives carry. Converting with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate; constant-time timing-safety stays the named
//! horizon.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/ed25519_to_x25519.rye

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const fe = @import("fe25519.rye");
const signer = @import("ed25519_sign.rye"); // the secret half names ed25519_sign's own clamped scalar; the selftest pins the public against it.
const x = @import("x25519.rye"); // the key agreement the secret half feeds; the selftest reaches its base point to pin the derived secret.

pub const ed25519_to_x25519_version = "20260816.104452";

pub const ConvertError = error{
    /// A hex or byte buffer handed to a selftest helper did not match its declared fixed length. The maps' own inputs are fixed 32-byte
    /// arrays, so this names the selftest helpers' contract, never a map itself.
    LengthMismatch,
};

/// The field element 1 — the additive identity's partner used on both sides of each map (1 + y, 1 − y, u − 1, u + 1).
fn field_one() fe.Field {
    return fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
}

/// Compare two 32-byte values. Used for the field-relationship postconditions and the selftest parity checks; equality here is a value check,
/// not a timing-safe compare (this file makes no timing claim).
fn same32(a: *const [32]u8, b: *const [32]u8) bool {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Is a canonical 32-byte field value all zero? Names the one degenerate input each map cannot invert (y = 1 for the forward map, u = −1 for
/// the reverse), so the exactness postcondition is asserted only where the inverse is defined.
fn is_zero32(b: *const [32]u8) bool {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) {
        if (b[i] != 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// eddsa_to_x25519 — map an Ed25519 public key to its X25519 public key. The low 255 bits carry the Edwards y-coordinate (from_bytes drops the
/// sign bit in bit 255), and RFC 7748 §4.1 lifts it to the Montgomery u-coordinate u = (1 + y)/(1 − y). The result is the same 32-byte X25519
/// public key the same secret scalar would produce through crypto/x25519.rye — one identity key, now usable for key agreement.
pub fn eddsa_to_x25519(ed_pub: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    const one = field_one();
    // invariant (construction): the field one is canonical — a single low limb set — so the numerator and denominator start from an exact 1.
    assert(one.l[0] == 1 and one.l[1] == 0 and one.l[2] == 0 and one.l[3] == 0 and one.l[4] == 0);

    const y = fe.from_bytes(ed_pub); // from_bytes masks bit 255, so the Ed25519 sign bit never enters the map.
    const num = fe.add(one, y); // 1 + y
    const den = fe.sub(one, y); // 1 − y
    const u = fe.mul(num, fe.invert(den));

    const den_bytes = fe.to_bytes(den);
    // invariant (postcondition): for every point but the degenerate y = 1 (den = 0), the inverse is exact — u·(1 − y) = 1 + y — so the map
    // is verified against the field, not assumed. The reduce-through-to_bytes makes both sides canonical before the value compare.
    assert(is_zero32(&den_bytes) or same32(&fe.to_bytes(fe.mul(u, den)), &fe.to_bytes(num)));
    return fe.to_bytes(u);
}

/// x25519_to_eddsa — map an X25519 u-coordinate back to an Edwards y-encoding. RFC 7748 §4.1's inverse y = (u − 1)/(u + 1); the result is
/// packed with sign bit 0 (bit 255 clear), the canonical encoding Monocypher's crypto_x25519_to_eddsa returns. A caller who needs a specific
/// x-sign restores it separately; the y-coordinate here is exact.
pub fn x25519_to_eddsa(u_coord: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    const one = field_one();
    // invariant (construction): the field one is canonical, so u − 1 and u + 1 start from an exact 1.
    assert(one.l[0] == 1 and one.l[1] == 0 and one.l[2] == 0 and one.l[3] == 0 and one.l[4] == 0);

    const u = fe.from_bytes(u_coord);
    const num = fe.sub(u, one); // u − 1
    const den = fe.add(u, one); // u + 1
    const y = fe.mul(num, fe.invert(den));

    const den_bytes = fe.to_bytes(den);
    // invariant (postcondition): for every u but the degenerate u = −1 (den = 0), the inverse is exact — y·(u + 1) = u − 1 — so the reverse
    // map is verified against the field. to_bytes reduces canonically, leaving bit 255 clear (y < p), the sign-0 encoding.
    assert(is_zero32(&den_bytes) or same32(&fe.to_bytes(fe.mul(y, den)), &fe.to_bytes(num)));
    return fe.to_bytes(y);
}

/// eddsa_secret_to_x25519 — the SECRET half of the birational map: derive the X25519 secret key from an Ed25519 seed. Where eddsa_to_x25519 turns a
/// peer's Ed25519 PUBLIC key into the X25519 public key to agree with, this turns one's OWN Ed25519 seed into the X25519 secret to agree with it.
/// The X25519 secret IS the Ed25519 clamped secret scalar a = clamp(SHA-512(seed)[0..32]) — the exact value crypto/ed25519_sign.rye already folds a
/// seed into to sign — so a Kumara identity holds ONE secret and both signs (Ed25519) and agrees (X25519) with it, never a second stored key. Adds
/// no new field mathematics and no new cryptography: it names crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's own secret scalar. Reads secret material — the signing
/// timing horizon covers this path, and deriving from the maintainer's own seed reaches the custody gate.
pub fn eddsa_secret_to_x25519(seed: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    return signer.secret_scalar(seed);
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A deterministic spread of test bytes — the same generator shape the sibling primitives use, seeded so each 32-byte scalar differs and none
/// is all-zero.
fn test_bytes(seed: u8) [32]u8 {
    var s: [32]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) s[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    s[0] |= 1; // guarantee a nonzero scalar
    return s;
}

/// Independent references: Zig's own edwards25519 and X25519. Used ONLY inside the selftest as second implementations to pin the maps. The
/// maps above never call std.crypto.
const ZEd = std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519;
const ZX = std.crypto.dh.X25519;
const ZEdSign = std.crypto.sign.Ed25519; // the deterministic Ed25519 keypair Zig converts, pinning the SECRET half of the map.

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. Published RFC 7748 §4.1 base-point relationship: the Edwards base point has y = 4/5, and (1 + 4/5)/(1 − 4/5) = 9 — exactly the X25519
    //    base u-coordinate. So eddsa_to_x25519(Edwards base) must be 9 on the nose, and x25519_to_eddsa(9) must recover the base's y.
    {
        const base_bytes = ZEd.basePoint.toBytes();
        var nine = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
        nine[0] = 9;
        if (!same32(&eddsa_to_x25519(&base_bytes), &nine)) return error.BasePointNotNine;

        // The base's y-encoding with the sign bit cleared is exactly what the reverse map returns for u = 9.
        var base_y = base_bytes;
        base_y[31] &= 0x7f;
        if (!same32(&x25519_to_eddsa(&nine), &base_y)) return error.ReverseBaseMismatch;
    }

    // 2. Independent-implementation parity across a deterministic spread of scalars. For each scalar the Edwards public key and the X25519
    //    public key are the SAME identity in two coordinate systems (both from Zig, both clamped identically), so the forward map must bridge
    //    them byte-for-byte — two independent implementations pinning eddsa_to_x25519.
    {
        var seed: u8 = 1;
        while (seed <= 24) : (seed += 1) {
            const sk = test_bytes(seed);
            const ed_pub = (try ZEd.basePoint.clampedMul(sk)).toBytes(); // s·B on the Edwards curve, same clamp X25519 uses.
            const x_pub = try ZX.recoverPublicKey(sk); // s over the Montgomery base 9, the X25519 public key.

            // The forward map turns the one identity's Edwards key into its X25519 key.
            if (!same32(&eddsa_to_x25519(&ed_pub), &x_pub)) return error.ForwardParityMismatch;

            // The reverse map recovers the Edwards y-coordinate (sign bit cleared) from the X25519 key.
            var ed_y = ed_pub;
            ed_y[31] &= 0x7f;
            if (!same32(&x25519_to_eddsa(&x_pub), &ed_y)) return error.ReverseParityMismatch;

            // Round-trip: reverse then forward returns the original u byte-for-byte (the forward map ignores the sign bit, so the recovered
            // y-encoding lifts back to exactly the starting u).
            const back = x25519_to_eddsa(&x_pub);
            if (!same32(&eddsa_to_x25519(&back), &x_pub)) return error.RoundTripMismatch;
        }
    }

    // 3. The SECRET half of the map. From an Ed25519 seed, eddsa_secret_to_x25519 derives the X25519 secret key; that secret's own X25519 public
    //    (x25519_base) must equal the forward map applied to the seed's Ed25519 public — the two ways to the identity's X25519 public agree,
    //    proving the secret and public halves name one keypair. Zig's independent X25519.KeyPair.fromEd25519, run over the same seed's Ed25519
    //    keypair, pins BOTH the derived secret and its public byte-for-byte.
    {
        var seed_i: u8 = 1;
        while (seed_i <= 24) : (seed_i += 1) {
            const seed = test_bytes(seed_i);
            const x_secret = eddsa_secret_to_x25519(&seed);
            const ed_pub = signer.derive_public(&seed);

            // Our two independent ways to the identity's X25519 public: base·secret through the ladder, and the forward map of the Ed25519 public.
            const pub_via_secret = x.x25519_base(&x_secret);
            const pub_via_map = eddsa_to_x25519(&ed_pub);
            if (!same32(&pub_via_secret, &pub_via_map)) return error.SecretPublicMismatch;

            // Independent oracle: Zig derives the same X25519 keypair from the same Ed25519 seed (clamp(SHA-512(seed)[0..32])).
            const ed_kp = try ZEdSign.KeyPair.generateDeterministic(seed);
            const mont = try ZX.KeyPair.fromEd25519(ed_kp);
            if (!same32(&x_secret, &mont.secret_key)) return error.SecretParityMismatch;
            if (!same32(&pub_via_secret, &mont.public_key)) return error.SecretPublicParityMismatch;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-ed25519-to-x25519: the birational Edwards↔Montgomery key conversion authored in pure Rye — one Ed25519 identity key " ++
        "made usable for X25519 key agreement, and back. Curve25519 and edwards25519 are the same curve in two coordinate systems, so RFC " ++
        "7748 §4.1's map u = (1 + y)/(1 − y) and its inverse y = (u − 1)/(u + 1) let one identity both sign and agree. eddsa_to_x25519 drops " ++
        "the Ed25519 sign bit, lifts y to the Montgomery u, and packs the 32-byte X25519 public key; x25519_to_eddsa lifts u back to a " ++
        "sign-0 Edwards y-encoding — each just one add, one subtract, and one inverse-multiply over the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye), " ++
        "no new field mathematics, and each verifies its own inverse against the field (u·(1 − y) = 1 + y) as a postcondition. The maps call " ++
        "no std.crypto. Parity holds against references runnable now: the published RFC 7748 §4.1 base-point relationship (the Edwards base's " ++
        "y = 4/5 maps to u = 9 exactly, and back), and our authored maps bridge Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519 public key and " ++
        "std.crypto.dh.X25519 public key byte-for-byte across twenty-four deterministic clamped scalars, with a reverse-then-forward " ++
        "round-trip recovering each u exactly. The secret half — eddsa_secret_to_x25519 — derives the X25519 secret from an Ed25519 seed (the " ++
        "clamped SHA-512 scalar the same key signs with), and its own X25519 public agrees with the forward map of that seed's public key, both " ++
        "pinned against Zig's independent std.crypto.dh.X25519.KeyPair.fromEd25519 across the same spread — so one identity holds ONE secret and " ++
        "both signs and agrees. Monocypher's own crypto_eddsa_to_x25519 / crypto_x25519_to_eddsa compute the same maps — its " ++
        "vendored source is a held fetch, so Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local over TEST keys — no real " ++
        "identity key, no network, no funds, no real device; constant-time timing-safety stays a named horizon, and converting the " ++
        "maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate. Kumara's one key now both signs and agrees.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
